    $astroNW_master$> The revolution now has almost everything. Copernicus supplied the bold idea; Kepler the exact laws; Galileo the decisive evidence and the new physics of motion. But one thing is still missing, the deepest thing of all: the **cause.** *Why* do the planets move in ellipses, sweep equal areas, obey $T^2 = a^3$? Kepler found *that* the planets obey his laws but never *why.* This chapter is about the mind that supplied it — **Isaac Newton** — and the single breathtaking idea with which he completed the revolution: **universal gravitation.** One force, acting everywhere, on everything, the same in the heavens as on the Earth — from which all three of Kepler's laws *follow*, and by which the very same pull that drops an apple holds the Moon in its orbit. In one stroke, Newton unified the heavens and the Earth, dissolved the two-thousand-year division between them, and revealed the universe as a single lawful system, comprehensible in its entirety to the human mind. This is the climax of the whole story.

In 1665, the plague came to England, and Cambridge closed its doors and sent its students home. Among them was a quiet, intense twenty-two-year-old named Isaac Newton, who returned to his family's farm at Woolsthorpe and there, over the next eighteen months — his *anni mirabiles*, his "miracle years" — did more to reshape human understanding than perhaps anyone before or since has done in so short a time. Alone, in rural isolation, he invented the calculus, made fundamental discoveries about light and color, and began to conceive the idea that would become universal gravitation.

There is a famous story — one that, unlike many, seems rooted in Newton's own recollections — that the seed came when he watched an apple fall. Not the cartoon version, where it bonks him on the head; the real insight was deeper. The apple falls *toward the Earth* — but *how far up does that pull reach?* To the treetops? The clouds? Or… all the way to the *Moon?* And if the force that pulls the apple down reaches the Moon — might that very same force be what *holds the Moon in its orbit*, continually bending its path into a circle instead of letting it fly off straight? For two thousand years, the terrestrial and the celestial had been utterly separate realms with separate laws. To suggest one force governs both — that the Moon is, in a sense, *falling*, just like the apple, only moving fast enough sideways to keep missing the Earth — was to propose that heaven and Earth obey *one physics.* Newton did not just have the idea. He *tested* it, with a calculation we will do below — and the numbers matched. He published the complete system in 1687, in arguably the most important scientific work ever written: the *Principia.*

{{image: Isaac Newton | Isaac Newton (1642–1727), who supplied the one thing the revolution still lacked: the cause. His law of universal gravitation — every mass attracting every other, weakening as the inverse square of distance — explained all three of Kepler's laws at once and unified the heavens and the Earth into a single physics.}}

## The three laws of motion

Before gravity, Newton's foundation: his **three laws of motion**, which define how forces produce motion — Galileo's physics of inertia, completed and made precise.

**First Law (inertia):** a body remains at rest, or moves in a straight line at constant speed, unless acted on by a net force. Uniform motion needs no cause; what needs a cause is any *change* in motion — including a change of *direction.* This is the key for astronomy. A planet on a curved orbit is continuously changing direction — so *a force must be acting on it*, continuously bending it from the straight line it would otherwise follow. Notice how completely this inverts two thousand years of intuition: to the ancients, eternal circular motion was the *natural* state of the heavens, needing no explanation. To Newton, *straight-line* motion is natural — and it is the *orbit* that demands explanation. The old question was "what keeps the planets moving?" The right question is "*what bends their motion into orbits?*"

**Second Law:** $F = ma$ — force equals mass times acceleration. This quantifies exactly how a force changes motion, and it is the engine that turns "there is a force" into "here is the exact orbit."

**Third Law:** for every action, an equal and opposite reaction. When the Sun pulls on a planet, the planet pulls *back* on the Sun equally — so the Sun too is tugged, orbiting the system's common center of mass, if only slightly. Gravity is always a two-way street. (Centuries later, the tiny wobble a planet induces in its star is exactly how we detect exoplanets around other suns.)

## Universal gravitation

Here is the central idea, one of the most important single sentences in the history of science:

> **Every mass attracts every other mass with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.**

$$F = \frac{G\, m_1 m_2}{r^2}$$

Three profound features are packed into this law. The single most important word is **universal**: *every* mass attracts *every other* — not just apples and the Earth, but all matter, everywhere, always. The apple attracts the Earth; the Sun attracts the planets; you attract the person next to you (imperceptibly); every atom attracts every other atom in the cosmos. There is no separate "celestial physics" — one gravity, everywhere, acting identically on a clod of earth and a distant star. This one word dissolves the two-thousand-year Aristotelian wall between the realms.

The force is **proportional to the masses** — which is why gravity is only noticeable when at least one object is enormous. And it **weakens as the inverse square of distance**: double the distance and the force drops to a quarter; ten times the distance, a hundredth. The inverse-square form is not arbitrary — it has a beautiful geometric rationale (an influence spreading equally in all directions dilutes over the surface of an expanding sphere, whose area grows as $r^2$), and it turns out to be *exactly* the dependence needed to produce Kepler's laws. A different law — inverse-cube, inverse-distance — would give different orbits that do not match the sky. Nature chose inverse-square, and that choice is written in the shapes of the planetary orbits.

## The apple and the Moon: the unification made concrete

Now the calculation at the heart of the whole revolution — Newton's test that the apple's gravity and the Moon's orbit are one force. The plan: take the acceleration of gravity at the Earth's surface (9.8 m/s², the falling apple). Weaken it by the inverse square of the Moon's distance — about **60 Earth-radii** — so it should be $60^2 \approx 3{,}600$ times weaker: about $2.7 \times 10^{-3}$ m/s². Then, *completely independently*, compute the acceleration the Moon actually has in its orbit, from pure geometry — its orbital radius and 27.3-day period, $a = 4\pi^2 r / T^2$: also about $2.7 \times 10^{-3}$ m/s². Slide out from the apple to the Moon and watch the two numbers meet:

```applemoon
```

**The two numbers match, to about one percent.** Two utterly independent quantities — surface gravity diluted by the inverse square, and the Moon's real orbital acceleration from its motion alone — are the same number. This is not coincidence; it is proof that **the force that pulls the apple to the ground and the force that holds the Moon in its orbit are one and the same force.** The Moon is, quite literally, falling — pulled by the same gravity as the apple — but moving so fast sideways that it continually *falls around* the Earth instead of hitting it. The ancient wall between the terrestrial and the celestial is demolished by a single calculation, checkable by anyone. Run the dilution yourself at any distance:

```example
apple-moon
```

Newton made "falling around the Earth" vivid with a beautiful thought experiment — the **cannonball.** Fire a cannonball horizontally from an impossibly high mountain. Too slow, and it arcs to the ground. Faster, and it lands farther away, the Earth's surface curving away beneath it. Fast enough, and the surface curves away at *exactly the rate the ball falls* — it never gets closer to the ground, and circles forever. Try it:

```newtoncannon
```

**That is an orbit: perpetual falling, moving sideways fast enough to keep missing the ground.** The Moon does exactly this; so does every satellite; so does the International Space Station, whose astronauts float not because gravity is absent but because they are in constant free fall. Newton conceived the artificial satellite three centuries before we launched one — and the same law sets the escape speed, about 11.2 km/s from Earth's surface, the wall every rocket ever launched has had to beat. From an apple in a Lincolnshire orchard to the price of leaving the planet: one law.

## Deriving Kepler's laws: gravity explains the harmony

Now Newton's supreme technical triumph, the thing that supplied the "cause" Kepler lacked: he *derived all three of Kepler's laws* from gravitation. Kepler's laws were not arbitrary facts; they were *what gravity requires.*

**The First Law (ellipses).** Newton proved — with the calculus he had invented for the purpose — that a body moving under an inverse-square attraction must follow a *conic section*, and for a bound orbit, an **ellipse with the central body at one focus.** The mysteries Kepler could only observe are explained: *why* an ellipse? Because that is the shape an inverse-square force necessarily produces. *Why* the Sun at a focus? Because the focus is precisely where the source of the force sits. Even the empty second focus that so puzzled Kepler is explained: it is simply a geometric feature of the curve — nothing sits there because nothing needs to. (And the unbound conics — parabolas, hyperbolas — are exactly the paths of escaping spacecraft and once-visiting interstellar comets.)

**The Second Law (equal areas).** This follows from gravity being a *central force* — always pointing straight toward the Sun, exerting no twist. A central force conserves angular momentum, and conservation of angular momentum is *mathematically equivalent* to sweeping equal areas in equal times. Tellingly, this part doesn't even need the inverse-square form — *any* central force gives equal areas; it is the first and third laws that pin down inverse-square specifically.

**The Third Law (the harmonic law).** Most beautifully, Newton showed $T^2 \propto a^3$ follows *specifically* from the inverse-square form — a different force law would give a different exponent. Kepler's mysterious harmonic law was gravity's signature all along, and Newton decoded it. And his derivation carried a spectacular bonus. Newton's exact form is:

$$T^2 = \frac{4\pi^2}{G M}\, a^3$$

The mass $M$ of the *central body* appears explicitly — which means that by measuring an orbit's period and size, you can *solve for the mass of the thing being orbited.* **You can weigh the Sun.** Plugging in Earth's orbit gives $1.99 \times 10^{30}$ kg — exactly the accepted value. Kepler's version gave only relative distances; Newton's version weighs worlds. From the orbit of a moon we weigh its planet; from a planet, its star; from stars circling the galactic center, the galaxy itself — which is how, much later, astronomers would discover that most of the galaxy's mass is invisible. Weigh a world yourself:

```example
weigh-the-sun
```

Step back and see what this means. Kepler's three laws — three separate, exact, *unexplained* empirical facts — are revealed as *three consequences of one cause.* The laws Kepler read off the sky, Newton read *out of gravity.* This is precisely the step from *description* to *understanding*, and it completes the revolution's chain: Copernicus's idea, Tycho's data, Kepler's laws, Galileo's evidence — and Newton's cause, explaining why it all had to be so.

## The sweep of success

The power of the theory showed in the astonishing *range* of things it explained beyond the planetary orbits. **The tides:** the Moon's gravity pulls harder on the near side of the Earth than the far side, and that *difference* stretches the oceans into two bulges — the two daily high tides (spring tides when Sun and Moon align, neap when perpendicular; the Moon dominates, its tidal effect about twice the Sun's, because tides depend on the difference in pull, which falls off faster than the pull itself). **The precession of the equinoxes:** the 26,000-year wobble Hipparchus discovered eighteen centuries earlier — described but unexplained in our precession chapter — finally had its cause: the Sun's and Moon's gravity torquing the spinning Earth's equatorial bulge. **The shape of the Earth:** Newton predicted our spinning planet must bulge at the equator — later confirmed by measurement. **Comets:** Newton's friend Edmond Halley used gravity to compute a comet's orbit and *predict its return* in 76 years; Halley's Comet duly returned in 1758, years after his death.

{{image: Principia | The title page of Newton's Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica (1687) — arguably the most important scientific work ever written. From three laws of motion and one law of gravity, it derived Kepler's laws, explained the tides and the precession of the equinoxes, and unified the heavens and the Earth under a single physics.}}

And the most dramatic triumph came a century and a half later. In the 1840s, astronomers found tiny irregularities in the orbit of Uranus — it was not quite where Newton's gravity said it should be. Rather than doubt the theory, Le Verrier and Adams *trusted* it, and calculated where an *unseen* planet must lie to produce the discrepancy. Astronomers pointed their telescopes at the predicted spot — and found **Neptune**, within a degree, in 1846. **Gravity had predicted an entire new planet before any human eye had seen it** — perhaps the most stunning confirmation any physical theory has ever received. Not merely fitting the known facts, but predicting new ones: for over two centuries, Newton's gravity reigned as the most successful theory in all of science.

{{image: Neptune | Neptune, photographed by Voyager 2. Its existence and position were calculated from Newton's gravity alone — from the tiny tugs it exerted on Uranus — before any telescope had knowingly seen it. Found in 1846 within a degree of the prediction, it remains perhaps the most stunning confirmation a physical theory has ever received.}}

## The honest limits: law without ultimate cause

In the spirit of honesty this course has kept throughout — with Ptolemy's success, with Copernicus's incompleteness — the limits must be stated. Newton gave the *law* of gravity — an exact account of *how* it acts — but not *what gravity is* or *why.* How does the Sun reach across 150 million kilometers of empty space to pull on the Earth, with nothing in between to carry the pull? This "action at a distance" troubled Newton deeply. His famous reply — *hypotheses non fingo*, "I frame no hypotheses" — meant he would give the exact mathematics and refuse to invent a story about gravity's ultimate nature, which he did not know. **So Newton, exactly like Kepler before him, gave exact law without ultimate cause.** Each generation finds a deeper law that explains the previous mystery — and poses its own.

And there was one tiny crack, reminiscent of Kepler's eight arcminutes: the orbit of Mercury precesses very slightly faster than Newton's gravity predicts — a minuscule, stubborn discrepancy. The deeper answer came in 1915 with Einstein's General Relativity, which reconceived gravity not as a force reaching across space but as the *curvature of spacetime itself* — matter tells space how to curve, curved space tells matter how to move. It explained Mercury's anomaly exactly and predicted effects Newton's theory could not (starlight bending past the Sun, confirmed in 1919). Crucially, Newton's gravity is *not wrong* — it is superbly accurate, still used to navigate every spacecraft — and it emerges from Einstein's theory as the correct approximation everywhere but the most extreme conditions. That is how science works, and it is the deepest lesson of the unit: a monumental, correct-to-superb-precision step — *and also* a rung on a ladder that keeps climbing.

## The revolution complete

Gather the whole arc, because with Newton the revolution that Copernicus began is *complete.* The ancient cosmos was a successful, reasonable, Earth-centered model. Copernicus moved the Earth — the bold idea. Kepler found the exact laws — breaking the circle. Galileo supplied the evidence and the new physics of motion — making a moving Earth visible and intelligible. And Newton supplied the *cause* — universal gravitation — from which all of Kepler's laws follow, by which the apple and the Moon are one phenomenon, and with which the heavens and the Earth become one physics.

**Here is the lesson to carry, the culmination of the entire two-thousand-year story.** The deepest thing Newton gave us is the vision of a *unified, comprehensible universe*: the discovery that beneath the endless variety of celestial and terrestrial phenomena lie a few universal laws, discoverable by the human mind, that govern the whole of nature everywhere the same. The same laws you can find in a falling apple govern the farthest planet — so by studying nature *here*, we learn the laws that hold *everywhere.* This is the founding vision of all physics, and the template for every great unification since: electricity and magnetism, space and time, the forces of matter. The apple and the Moon obey one gravity; heaven and Earth are one physics; the cosmos is lawful and knowable. That is the revolution, complete — and it stands among the very greatest achievements of the human mind.

One question remains — one that seemed permanently beyond reach even after Newton. We know *where* the celestial bodies are, and now *why they move.* But *what are the stars actually made of?* In 1835 the philosopher Auguste Comte declared the chemical composition of the stars the very type of the eternally unknowable — something humanity would *never* learn. He was spectacularly wrong, within decades. The finale of the course is the discovery that proved him so: the spectroscope, and the astonishing fact that starlight carries within it the chemical signature of its source. The birth of astrophysics awaits.

## Further reading

The *Principia* itself is famously difficult (Newton wrote it in the geometric style deliberately), but any good treatment of Newton's laws and gravitation covers this chapter's ground. The story of the Neptune prediction — Le Verrier, Adams, and the night the planet was found within a degree of the calculation — is one of the great narratives in science, and Newton's own strange, solitary, obsessive life rewards a good biography.$astroNW_master$,
